CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: the diff and surrounding context alone are not enough to say.
The caching, preloading, and cancellation mechanisms are clear, but this context does not include the registration/build path proving that the new service worker controls real clients. Observable effects also depend on runtime asset requests, cache contents, and concurrent component lifecycles.

The change introduces a browser service worker that intercepts image asset GETs, serves successful responses from a deployment-versioned cache, supports background preloading, and allows component teardown to cancel pending image fetches. It also adds a production Compose shutdown target. The supplied diff does not show service-worker registration, so whether these mechanisms affect a real installation cannot be established here.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When the service worker controls a client, matching original and thumbnail asset GETs are handled cache-first; successful network responses are stored and later requests for the same URL can be returned from browser cache.
  • Photo-viewer preloading now asks the service worker to fetch and cache adjacent image URLs rather than creating browser Image objects directly.
  • When an image thumbnail or viewer image is torn down, it can notify the service worker to abort a pending fetch for that URL, reducing work for images no longer needed.
  • Activating a new service-worker version claims existing clients and removes every Cache Storage entry whose name is not the current deployment cache name.
  • Developers can now stop and remove the production Docker Compose stack and its orphaned containers through the new prod-down Make target.

Investigate
  • Trace where this service worker is built, registered, scoped, updated, and allowed to control clients; then identify the UI routes that mount the changed photo and thumbnail components.
  • Inspect asset endpoint authentication and cache-key requirements, including whether identical asset URLs can produce user-specific responses or change without a deployment version change.
  • Analyze overlapping requests for the same URL across thumbnails, the viewer, browser fetch interception, and explicit preloads, especially how URL-keyed AbortControllers are replaced and removed.
  • Determine cache ownership for this origin and whether other application features or service workers use separate named caches that must survive activation.

Uncertain
  • The supplied files do not establish every HTTP/UI route that mounts the changed photo-viewer or thumbnail components and therefore emits preload or cancellation messages.
  • It is not shown whether there are additional callers of the messaging helpers or direct consumers of the service-worker asset-loading path beyond the changed components.
  • The diff does not show service-worker registration, successful installation, browser support, or whether a client is controlled when image requests occur.
  • The authorization and tenancy boundary of the intercepted asset URLs is not shown, so it is unknown whether cached responses are safely reusable across sessions for the same origin and URL.
  • The expected behavior for mutable assets, HTTP cache directives, failed Cache Storage writes, and service-worker lifetime around the unawaited cache.put operation is not established by the supplied context.

